 ## Overview
-Piecewise Constant Approximation Inverse is a method for computing the inverse of the density integral along a ray, used to determine sample positions in inverse opacity sampling [S1, unofficial]. It assumes that the density $\sigma_{\mathbf{r}}$ is constant within each discrete sample interval $[t_{j-1}, t_j]$ [S1, unofficial]. This approach differs from its sibling, [Piecewise Linear Approximation Inverse](2649-piecewise-linear-approximation-inverse.md), by treating density as a step function rather than a linearly varying one, resulting in a linear equation for the ray parameter $t$ instead of a quadratic one [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Inverse Opacity Calculation](1099-inverse-opacity-calculation.md) by providing an explicit closed-form solution for the ray parameter given a target cumulative density value [S1, unofficial].
+Piecewise Constant Approximation Inverse computes the inverse of the density integral along a ray to determine sample positions in inverse opacity sampling [S1, unofficial]. It assumes the density $\sigma_{\mathbf{r}}$ is constant within each discrete sample interval $[t_{j-1}, t_j]$ [S1, unofficial]. This approach differs from its sibling, [Piecewise Linear Approximation Inverse](2649-piecewise-linear-approximation-inverse.md), by treating density as a step function rather than a linearly varying one, resulting in a linear equation for the ray parameter $t$ instead of a quadratic one [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Inverse Opacity Calculation](1099-inverse-opacity-calculation.md) by providing an explicit closed-form solution for the ray parameter given a target cumulative density value [S1, unofficial].
 
